4080: history of artificial intelligence began in antiquity, with myth
40c0: s, stories and rumors of artificial beings endowed with intellig
4100: ence or consciousness by master craftsmen. The seeds of modern A
4140: I were planted by classical philosophers who attempted to descri
4180: be the process of human thinking as the mechanical manipulation 
41c0: of symbols. This work culminated in the invention of the program
4200: mable digital computer in the 1940s, a machine based on the abst
4240: ract essence of mathematical reasoning. This device and the idea
4280: s behind it inspired a handful of scientists to begin seriously 
42c0: discussing the possibility of building an electronic brain. The 
4300: field of AI research was founded at a workshop held on the campu
4340: s of Dartmouth College during the summer of 1956. The attendees 
4380: became the leaders of AI research for decades. Many of them pred
43c0: icted that machines as intelligent as humans would exist within 
4400: a generation, and they were given millions of dollars to make th
4440: is vision come true. Eventually it became obvious that they had 
4480: grossly underestimated the difficulty of the project. In 1974, i
44c0: n response to criticism from James Lighthill and ongoing pressur
4500: e from Congress, the United States and British governments cut o
4540: ff exploratory research in AI. The next few years would later be
4580:  called an AI winter. In the early 1980s, AI research was revive
45c0: d by the commercial success of expert systems, a form of AI prog
4600: ram that simulated the knowledge and analytical skills of human 
4640: experts. By 1985, the market for AI had reached over a billion d
4680: ollars. At the same time, Japan's fifth generation computer proj
46c0: ect inspired the United States and British governments to restor
4700: e funding for academic research. However, beginning with the col
4740: lapse of the Lisp Machine market in 1987, AI once again fell int
4780: o disrepute. A second AI winter began. The field of machine lear
47c0: ning would be transformed by the rise of deep learning in the 20
4800: 10s. Neural networks had been studied since the 1940s, but they 
4840: were limited by the computational power available. The developme
4880: nt of graphics processing units enabled researchers to train muc
48c0: h larger networks. In 2012, a deep neural network called AlexNet
4900:  achieved a breakthrough in image recognition, dramatically outp
4940: erforming traditional approaches. This success sparked a wave of
4980:  interest in deep learning. Researchers began applying neural ne
49c0: tworks to natural language processing, speech recognition, game 
4a00: playing, and many other domains. The transformer architecture, i
4a40: ntroduced in 2017, proved particularly powerful for language tas
4a80: ks. Models based on transformers, such as BERT and GPT, achieved
4ac0:  state-of-the-art results on numerous benchmarks. The scaling hy
4b00: pothesis suggested that larger models trained on more data would
4b40:  continue to improve, and this prediction was borne out by the d
4b80: evelopment of increasingly large language models throughout the 
4bc0: late 2010s and early 2020s. These models demonstrated surprising
4c00:  capabilities in generating text, answering questions, writing c
4c40: ode, and even reasoning about complex topics. The release of Cha
4c80: tGPT in November 2022 brought conversational AI to widespread pu
4cc0: blic attention and sparked intense interest in the potential app
4d00: lications and risks of artificial intelligence. Researchers cont
4d40: inue to explore new architectures, training methods, and applica
4d80: tions for AI systems, while policymakers grapple with questions 
4dc0: about regulation, safety, and the societal impact of these power
4e00: ful technologies. The development of reinforcement learning from
4e40:  human feedback proved to be a key technique for aligning langua
4e80: ge models with human preferences. This approach, combined with c
4ec0: onstitutional AI methods and other safety techniques, has become
4f00:  central to the responsible deployment of large language models.
4f40:  Meanwhile, multimodal models that can process images, audio, an
4f80: d video alongside text have opened new frontiers in artificial i
4fc0: ntelligence capabilities. Computer vision has made remarkable pr
5000: ogress through convolutional neural networks and vision transfor
5040: mers. Object detection, image segmentation, and image generation
5080:  models like DALL-E and Stable Diffusion have transformed creati
50c0: ve industries and enabled new applications in healthcare, autono
5100: mous driving, and scientific research. Robotics has benefited fr
5140: om advances in reinforcement learning and sim-to-real transfer, 
5180: allowing robots to learn complex manipulation tasks. Natural lan
51c0: guage processing has progressed from simple bag-of-words models 
